STEP 6: ROTATION SUPPORT IN ANALYTIC EVALUATION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The torus excitation has a rotation quaternion that updates each frame 
(elapsed_time * 0.5 rad/s around Y). The analytic SDF evaluation and 
normal computation must apply the inverse rotation to transform the 
ray point into the object's local space.

Quaternion rotation helpers needed in WGSL:

```wgsl
fn quat_rotate(p: vec3f, q: vec4f) -> vec3f {
    let u = q.xyz;
    let s = q.w;
    return 2.0 * dot(u, p) * u + (s*s - dot(u, u)) * p + 2.0 * s * cross(u, p);
}

fn quat_inverse_rotate(p: vec3f, q: vec4f) -> vec3f {
    // Conjugate quaternion (negate xyz)
    return quat_rotate(p, vec4f(-q.xyz, q.w));
}

fn apply_inverse_rotation(p: vec3f, q: vec4f) -> vec3f {
    // If quaternion is identity (0,0,0,1), skip
    if (abs(q.w - 1.0) < 0.001 && length(q.xyz) < 0.001) { return p; }
    return quat_inverse_rotate(p, q);
}

fn apply_rotation(n: vec3f, q: vec4f) -> vec3f {
    if (abs(q.w - 1.0) < 0.001 && length(q.xyz) < 0.001) { return n; }
    return quat_rotate(n, q);
}
```

