The counter-proposal is better than my original prompt in every way that matters. Let me reason through each point.

**ObserverCell at 24 bytes** — yes. The observer never reads phi. Stripping it gives 14.7MB total observer read surface which fits in GPU L2 cache. Every trilinear sample during ray march becomes a cache hit instead of DRAM fetch. This is a measurable performance win.

**Mandelbulb can't be in derive** — I should have caught this. 614K cells × 4 Mandelbulbs × (pow + acos + atan2 + 5 iterations) = ~123M heavy-math instructions per frame just for stamping. The derive pass should stamp SIMPLE shapes (sphere, box, cylinder, torus) and the observer evaluates Mandelbulbs analytically during ray march with bounding sphere culling. Two entity categories: field entities (stamped) and ray entities (observer-only).

**Room SDF half-extents** — correct. 80×0.125 = 10m. Walls need thickness. half = (4.65, 1.3, 4.65) gives 0.35m shell = ~3 cells thick.

**Session 1 = room only** — the right call. Prove the architecture works before adding content. If a lit, patterned room with camera movement shows zero flicker, the SoA foundation is solid.

